# Contact Centre AI (E1): SECRET values.
#
# Copy to `.env.secrets`, restrict it to the deployment operators, and never commit it (it is
# gitignored). Keeping secrets in a separate file from `.env` is what lets `.env` be reviewed,
# diffed and shared while these values are not.
#
# Every variable here is resolved in THREE states by the code that reads it. UNSET and
# SET-AND-EMPTY are different: an operator who set a variable to empty expressed an intent that
# names nothing, and it fails closed rather than inheriting the unset default. A templated
# deployment that substitutes a missing secret with "" therefore fails loudly instead of serving
# unauthenticated.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Inbound: the bearer other services must present to this service's S2S routes.
# UNSET is the documented zero-secret loopback dev opening. SET-AND-EMPTY is a 503.
#
# It authenticates a calling SERVICE and NO end user, so setting it closes the S2S routes and
# nothing else: it does not make /v1/triage or /v1/personas authenticated and it does not relax
# the loopback exposure guard, which reads the identity binding and never this variable.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CONTACT_S2S_TOKEN=REPLACE_WITH_SECRET

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Outbound (rule R8): the credentials this service presents to the Hrz7 console.
# Deliberately DISTINCT from the inbound token above: one is what this service accepts, the
# other is what it can assert elsewhere, and a single shared value would conflate them.
#
# HUMAN_REVIEW_S2S_TOKEN is required for any non-loopback console and refused at client construction
# when absent, so an escalation never leaves unauthenticated. HUMAN_REVIEW_S2S_SIGNING_KEY signs the
# propagated actor; when unset the signed-actor pair is omitted rather than sent unsigned.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
HUMAN_REVIEW_S2S_TOKEN=REPLACE_WITH_SECRET
HUMAN_REVIEW_S2S_SIGNING_KEY=REPLACE_WITH_SECRET

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Outbound (rules R1 and R3, plus the self-service action path): the credentials this service
# presents to Hrz1 (the guardrail every inbound turn is screened through), Hrz2 (the governed
# index a suggestion is grounded in) and the MCP action catalog. ONE pair covers all three:
# they are siblings in this catalog reached over one shared transport
# (`adapters/gcp/_s2s.py`), not three separate trust relationships. Outbound like the HUMAN_REVIEW_S2S_*
# pair above, and for the same reason distinct from the inbound CONTACT_S2S_TOKEN. The URLs
# they authenticate against are GUARDRAIL_GATEWAY_URL, KNOWLEDGE_BASE_URL and
# CONTACT_TOOL_CATALOG_URL, which live in `.env` because they are not secret.
#
# They fail exactly like the HUMAN_REVIEW_S2S_* pair above, and for the same reasons.
#
# S2S_TOKEN is required for any non-loopback sibling and refused BEFORE the request leaves
# when absent, so a screen, a retrieval or an action call never departs unauthenticated. A
# loopback sibling is the offline zero-secret posture and needs none. S2S_SIGNING_KEY signs
# the propagated actor; when unset the signed-actor pair is OMITTED rather than sent unsigned, so
# a receiver is never handed an actor assertion it has no way to verify.
#
# EMPTIED is refused for both, wherever the sibling is. That refusal is the commons' own, in
# `hex_service_kit.s2s.client_headers`, which resolves the two states itself: a header builder
# that strips a value before it tests it cannot tell a blank credential from an absent one, so
# the distinction has to be drawn where the read happens.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
S2S_TOKEN=REPLACE_WITH_SECRET
S2S_SIGNING_KEY=REPLACE_WITH_SECRET
